- finding_id: I5
  title: Security comment claims tracing that doesn't exist
  weight: 3
  phase_ideal: reviewer
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: No phase flagged misleading SECURITY comments claiming logging exists
    when it doesn't. Reviewer noted missing logging but didn't connect it to false
    documentation claims.
- finding_id: I6
  title: Non-ASCII tenant header silently treated as missing
  weight: 8
  phase_ideal: tea
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: No test was written for non-ASCII tenant header values. TEA's security
    tests don't cover this edge case. Reviewer noted to_str() rejects non-visible
    ASCII in Authorization header but didn't flag the silent failure mode for X-Tenant-ID.
- finding_id: I7
  title: "CWE-209 test gap \u2014 no body assertion"
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: tea
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: TEA wrote CWE-209 tests but the ground truth says they only check status
    code, not response body content. No phase flagged this gap.
- finding_id: I8
  title: CORS preflight test doesn't verify CORS headers
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: tea
  caught: true
  caught_by: reviewer
  evidence: 'Reviewer flagged: ''[LOW] CORS preflight test... asserts 200 || 204 but...
    The test doesn''t actually verify CORS headers are returned.'''
- finding_id: I9
  title: "Wrong log levels \u2014 error! for 4xx client errors"
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: reviewer
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: Reviewer flagged missing logging (I4) but since no logging was implemented,
    the wrong-log-level issue (error! for 4xx) was not encountered or flagged.
- finding_id: I10
  title: CORS allows Any origin/method/header (CWE-942)
  weight: 8
  phase_ideal: dev
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: TEA's security tests mention 'no wildcard origin' and reviewer noted the
    empty origins list, but neither flagged CWE-942 explicitly. The reviewer actually
    noted the empty list means no origin is allowed and called it 'correct for security,'
    missing that the actual implementation uses Any.
